<p><strong>To start a salon or beauty parlour in Nepal, define your services and customers, secure a location and the required registrations, hire and train stylists, and set up booking, payments and reporting before opening day. Check registration and licensing rules with the relevant Nepali authorities first; this guide covers day-to-day operations.</strong></p>
<p>This is an operations guide, not legal or tax advice. Confirm registration, licence, health and tax rules with the relevant authorities and your accountant.</p>
<h2>1. Choose your focus</h2>
<p>Hair, skin, nails, bridal, or a mix? A parlour that tries to do everything on day one can struggle to do any of it well. Decide your core services and price them.</p>
<h2>2. Build the team around the chair</h2>
<p>Your chairs and your stylists limit your revenue. Plan shifts and breaks, and track which stylist did which service. See <a href="/features/staff-management/">staff management</a>.</p>
<h2>3. Take bookings online from the start</h2>
<p>Clients expect to book without a call, especially for festival and wedding-season dates. Read <a href="/blog/salon-booking-system-nepal/">how to take salon bookings without phone calls</a> and <a href="/blog/beauty-parlour-software-nepal/">what to look for in beauty parlour software</a>.</p>
<h2>4. Set rules for discounts and money</h2>
<p>Decide who may discount and who approves. Record payments by method from day one. See <a href="/features/discount-management/">discount management</a> and <a href="/features/payment-tracking/">payment tracking</a>.</p>
<h2>5. Add packages and loyalty</h2>
<p>Bridal packages, facial courses and prepaid cards bring cash in early. Track them with <a href="/features/packages/">packages</a> and <a href="/features/memberships/">memberships</a>.</p>
<h2>6. Bring lapsed clients back</h2>
<p>Most parlours lose regulars quietly. <a href="/features/outreach/">Outreach</a> emails clients who have stopped visiting and asks for reviews.</p>
<h2>7. Plan for a second branch</h2>
<p>Pick software that scales. See the <a href="/blog/best-salon-software-nepal/">guide to choosing salon software</a> and the <a href="/solutions/salon/">salon solution</a>.</p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <h3>How do I start a beauty parlour business in Nepal?</h3>
      <p>Choose your services and customers, find a location, check registration and licence rules with the relevant authorities, hire and train stylists and beauticians, and set up bookings, payments and reporting from day one.</p>
      <h3>What does a new salon need to run well?</h3>
      <p>A clear service menu, trained staff, an online booking system that prevents double-bookings, a payment record by method and a rule for approving discounts.</p>
      <h3>Should a new beauty parlour take bookings online?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Online booking captures customers outside opening hours and avoids missed calls, and it records every booking in one place.</p>
      <h3>How do I plan for growth?</h3>
      <p>Choose software that works for one branch and many, so adding a second location does not mean changing systems.</p>
      
